<h2>Maintenance for children inside the DV case</h2>
<p>Section 20 is the workhorse. It requires monetary relief to be adequate, fair, reasonable and consistent with the standard of living the aggrieved person is accustomed to, and it reaches the expenses children actually generate: school and tuition fees, medical care, and the ordinary costs of growing up. Two practice points follow. First, plead the children's expenses distinctly — a consolidated figure invites a consolidated cut, while an itemised schedule of fees and costs anchored to receipts is hard to dismiss. Second, where a maintenance case under Section 144 BNSS or Section 26 HMA is also running, disclose it; the court will set off overlapping amounts, and candour protects the order from later attack.</p>

<h2>Custody under Section 21: fast, interim, protective</h2>
<p>The distinctive value of Section 21 is speed. A guardianship petition takes its course; the DV Magistrate can pass a temporary custody order at any stage of the hearing of the application, because the statute's premise is that a child in a violent household may need separation from the respondent now, not after a custody trial. The order is expressly temporary — it holds the position until the Family Court decides custody in the ordinary jurisdiction — and visitation is regulated within it, including by refusing visits that would harm the child. In Delhi, Magistrates commonly route contested visitation through supervised arrangements, and the existence of a Section 21 order is routinely placed before the Family Court when the full custody question is later tried.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Boys above eighteen:</strong> the DV Act's child-specific reliefs run to minors. An adult son is outside Section 2(b); an adult unmarried daughter's maintenance is pursued under the general maintenance law rather than Section 21 custody. Transition planning matters when a child in the case approaches majority — maintenance components should be restructured before the birthday, not after.</p>
</div>

<h2>Who applies for the child</h2>
<p>The application under Section 12 is the mother's, and the children's reliefs travel inside it. Where the mother is unable to act, the Act's machinery — the Protection Officer, or any person on the aggrieved person's behalf with leave — can set the process in motion. The respondent array follows the ordinary DV Act rules: the persons in the domestic relationship against whom the allegations are made. The children themselves are beneficiaries of the orders, not litigants, and courts keep them out of the courtroom unless their interaction is genuinely required.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>Checklist for a mother framing a DV application with children: identify each child with age in the application; itemise child-specific expenses under Section 20 with documents; seek Section 21 custody expressly if separation from the respondent is needed, with a concrete visitation proposal; ask for protection-order terms covering the children's school and daycare; and disclose every parallel maintenance or custody proceeding.</p>
</div>

